# The build context deliberately *includes* .git: `go build` reads the VCS revision from it
# and stamps it into the binary, which is what /meta reports and what makes a run
# certifiable (docs/design/measurement-contract.md §11 and §13). An image built without it
# serves an unstamped binary and every run against it is uncertifiable — the failure looks
# like a harness bug.
#
# **The rule that follows from that: this file may exclude only paths git already ignores.**
#
# Go decides `vcs.modified` by running `git status --porcelain` inside the build context and
# testing whether the output is empty. Excluding a *tracked* path does not hide it from that
# check — it makes git report the file as deleted, so the context reads as a modified tree
# and the binary is stamped `modified=true` even when the host checkout is spotless. The
# manifest refuses `service_source_modified` at `local`, the floor of the ladder, so the
# effect is not a cosmetic flag: it makes the production-oriented image uncertifiable by
# construction, and every run against it unquotable at any level.
#
# That is not hypothetical — an earlier version of this file excluded `docs/`, `test/`,
# `.github/` and `*.md`, which put 1,330 deleted paths in the context. The cost of the rule
# is a large context, because `docs/measurements/` is tracked and holds ~108 MB of result
# data; everything else tracked is under 2 MB. `make image-provenance` is the control that
# proves the stamp is honest, and `make build-context-check` fails if anything tracked is
# excluded here again.
#
# Every entry below is matched by .gitignore. Keep it that way.
bin/
dist/
coverage/
coverage.out
test/results/
deploy/observability/targets/
.env
.env.*
.vscode/
.idea/
*.log
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
